plan:
Here is the exact plan and the code to set up a **GitHub Action AI Controller**. 

This allows you to comment instructions (or open an issue) right inside GitHub, and a GitHub Actions runner will take your instructions, pass them to an LLM, modify your repo files automatically, and push the changes—acting just like Zaro.

### Plan:
1. Create a GitHub Action workflow file (`.github/workflows/ai-worker.yml`) that triggers whenever an issue comment or workflow dispatch occurs.
2. The action uses a Node.js script to read your prompt, call the AI API, rewrite/update your files, and commit/push the changes straight back to your repository.

### Code to apply:

Create this file in your repository at `.github/workflows/ai-worker.yml`:

```yaml
name: AI Worker Automation

on:
  workflow_dispatch:
    inputs:
      instructions:
        description: 'What would you like the AI worker to do?'
        required: true
        type: string
      target_file:
        description: 'Target file path to modify (e.g., worker.js)'
        required: true
        type: string

jobs:
  run-ai-worker:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: write
    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Node.js
        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: '18'

      - name: Run AI Patching Script
        env:
          AI_API_KEY: ${{ secrets.AI_API_KEY }}
          TARGET_FILE: ${{ inputs.target_file }}
          INSTRUCTIONS: ${{ inputs.instructions }}
        run: |
          node -e '
          const fs = require("fs");
          const path = require("path");

          async function execute() {
              const apiKey = process.env.AI_API_KEY;
              const targetFile = process.env.TARGET_FILE;
              const instructions = process.env.INSTRUCTIONS;

              if (!apiKey) {
                  console.error("Missing AI_API_KEY secret.");
                  process.exit(1);
              }

              const fullPath = path.resolve(targetFile);
              if (!fs.existsSync(fullPath)) {
                  console.error(`Target file not found: ${targetFile}`);
                  process.exit(1);
              }

              const fileContent = fs.readFileSync(fullPath, "utf8");
              console.log(`Processing ${targetFile} with instructions: "${instructions}"`);

              const response = await fetch("https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions", {
                  method: "POST",
                  headers: {
                      "Content-Type": "application/json",
                      "Authorization": `Bearer ${apiKey}`
                  },
                  body: JSON.stringify({
                      model: "gpt-4o",
                      messages: [
                          { role: "system", content: "You are an expert e-commerce backend engineer. Return ONLY the raw updated code block for the target file, with no markdown code fences and no explanations." },
                          { role: "user", content: `Instructions: ${instructions}\n\nExisting Code:\n${fileContent}` }
                      ],
                      temperature: 0.1
                  })
              });

              const data = await response.json();
              if (!data.choices || !data.choices[0]) {
                  console.error("AI API Error:", JSON.stringify(data));
                  process.exit(1);
              }

              let updatedCode = data.choices[0].message.content.trim();
              // Clean up markdown fences if the model accidentally included them
              updatedCode = updatedCode.replace(/^```[a-z]*\n?/i, "").replace(/```\s*$/, "");

              fs.writeFileSync(fullPath, updatedCode, "utf8");
              console.log("File successfully updated by AI worker.");
          }
          execute();
          '

      - name: Commit and push changes
        run: |
          git config --global user.name "AI Worker Bot"
          git config --global user.email "bot@bargain-drop.online"
          git add .
          git diff --quiet && git diff --staged --quiet || (git commit -m "AI Worker: Applied updates via workflow dispatch" && git push)
```

### How to use it:
1. Add your AI API key as a repository secret in GitHub under Settings > Secrets and variables > Actions, named `AI_API_KEY`.
2. Go to the **Actions** tab in your GitHub repository, select **AI Worker Automation**, and click **Run workflow**.
3. Type your instructions and the target file path, and the GitHub runner will execute the changes and push them live automatically.

